+### Phase 4: stream consumer (make stream)
+
+Work:
+- `pipeline/stream/consume.py`: an SSE client with `urllib` (standard library). It parses `event:` and
+  `data:` lines, and keeps the SSE `id:` field as the resume cursor. Takes `--url` and `--duration`.
+- Reconnects with `Last-Event-ID` when the connection drops (decision `stream-resume`).
+- Each micro-batch is written to `lake/stream_edit_raw/` through dlt with: the event fields, `meta_id`
+  (decision `stream-identity`), `arrival_seq` (consumer counter, monotonic), `window_start`, `window_end`,
+  `watermark_before` and `is_late` (decision `lateness-watermark`, decision `late-flag-in-fact`).
+- Order of writes per micro-batch: (1) write the batch file, (2) write the cursor file with a temp name and
+  rename it. A restart reads the cursor, then dedupes against `meta_id` already in the lake before it writes.
+- Stream clock: `window_start = floor(event_time to 5 min)`; `event_time` is `meta.dt` (UTC).
+- Late rule: `is_late = watermark_before > window_end + 10 minutes`, strictly greater. `watermark_before` is
+  the running maximum of `event_time` over rows already landed, in `arrival_seq` order, before the current
+  event. The rule is evaluated once, at first arrival.
+
+TDD note: write the tests first.
+- SSE parser unit test with fixture text: multi-line `data:`, comment lines, and `id:` lines.
+- Lateness unit test on a fixed sequence: an event 10 min 1 s past its window end is late; exactly 10 min is not.
+- Dedupe unit test: the same `meta_id` twice lands once.
+- Integration (against the replay on `localhost:8099`): kill the consumer after 1/3 of `DURATION`, restart,
+  and compare: unique `meta_id` count equals the count of distinct events in the replay; no duplicate; the
+  late count equals an uninterrupted run over the same replay.
+- Replay speed: if the replay server takes a speed option, run it at 1x and 10x and compare the late count.
+  If it does not, a unit test uses the same events with different wall-clock gaps and checks the same late count.
+
+Exit: all four unit tests pass; the integration restart test passes; `make stream DURATION=<s>` works.
+
+### Phase 5: stream star schema and window mart for Question B
+
+Work:
+- `stg_stream_edits`: typed view of `stream_edit_raw`. `actor_type` is `bot` when the `bot` flag is true,
+  otherwise `human` (decision `edit-classification`). `wiki` is the event's `wiki` (the `dbname`, for
+  example `enwiki`). `net_bytes = length.new - length.old`, NULL when either side is NULL.
+- `fct_edit` (incremental, `unique_key = meta_id`, merge): grain is one change event. Holds every consumed
+  event, late or not, with `is_late`. Edit types: `edit` and `new` (decision `edit-classification`).
+- `late_events` (table): the rows of `fct_edit` where `is_late`. Not a copy in the consumer.
+- `mart_edit_windows_5min` (incremental, `delete+insert` on `window_start`): grain is 5-minute window x wiki
+  x namespace x actor type. Columns: `edit_count`, `net_bytes_added`. Built from on-time rows only.
+- `dim_wiki` from `stg_wiki_sites`, so the `en.wikipedia.org` filter maps to `enwiki` (decision
+  `edit-classification`).
+
+TDD note: singular tests first.
+- `fct_edit` key `meta_id` is unique and not null.
+- `mart_edit_windows_5min` has no rows with `is_late = true` (test on the sum of edits for late rows = 0).
+- Sum of `edit_count` in the mart equals the on-time edit count in `fct_edit`.
+- Rebuilding `mart_edit_windows_5min` twice (incremental) gives the same rows.
