+### B-1: a run ends at DURATION, not after five failed reconnects
+
+Choice: option (a) of B-1. The run keeps reconnecting until its end. The cycle-5 failure bound (MF-10) is removed.
+
+- The consumer reconnects after every end of an SSE body and after every failed connect. It stops only at one of three
+  graceful ends: `DURATION expired`, `SIGINT`, or `upstream end signal` (HTTP 204 answered to a reconnect).
+- A failed reconnect attempt is still defined as in MF-10. A failed attempt does not end the run. The consumer counts
+  consecutive failed attempts, resets the count when a reconnect delivers a new event, and writes the count to its exit
+  line.
+- The wait between attempts is the SSE `retry:` value or 1 second, read from an injected sleep.
+- At a graceful end the consumer flushes the open micro-batch (batch file first, then cursor file), logs its reason and
+  its failed-attempt count, and exits with status 0.
+- Phase 4 comparisons when the replay sends no HTTP 204: both comparison runs use a `DURATION` longer than the replay's
+  last event. Both runs end at `DURATION expired`. The comparison requires equal `edit` plus `new` counts, equal `is_late`
+  sets per `meta_id`, and equal exit reasons. The failed-attempt count is recorded and not compared. A replay that never
+  sends HTTP 204 makes each run last its full `DURATION`; Phase 4 records that in its log.
+- Decisions `cd-1-stream-resume` and `cd-1-stream-micro-batch` are updated with this rule.
+- Rationale: the spec says `make stream DURATION=<seconds>` consumes the stream for that long. A failure bound ends the
+  run before `DURATION` with status 0, and no decision or deferral allows that.
+- Test `tests/test_stream_consumer.py::test_no_end_signal_stops_after_bounded_reconnects` is renamed to
+  `tests/test_stream_consumer.py::test_no_end_signal_runs_to_duration`, with two cases, both on an injected clock and sleep:
+  - Case 1: the fixture sends events 1 to 120 and closes the body cleanly. Every later reconnect is refused. Assert that all
+    120 events are in the lake before the exit, that the consumer keeps reconnecting until the fake clock reaches
+    `DURATION`, that the exit status is 0, that the logged reason is `DURATION expired`, and that the logged failed-attempt
+    count equals the attempts made.
+  - Case 2: the fixture sends events 1 to 120, then four refused reconnects, then events 121 to 240 and a clean close, then
+    refused reconnects. Assert that the failed-attempt count resets at events 121 to 240, that the run ends at
+    `DURATION expired`, and that all 240 events are in the lake.
