@@ -0,0 +1,8 @@
+---
+decision_id: cd-1-stream-micro-batch
+status: proposed
+question: How large is a stream micro-batch, and when is it flushed?
+choice: Flush every 500 events or every 10 seconds of wall-clock time, whichever comes first, and once more on a graceful end before the process exits. A graceful end is one of three triggers: DURATION expires, SIGINT, or the replay's end signal (HTTP 204 answered to a reconnect). Failed reconnects do not end the run (see cd-1-stream-resume). A clean close of the SSE body alone is a disconnect, and it does not end the run. Each flush is one dlt append load into stream_edit_raw. A hard kill (SIGKILL) gets no final flush. A batch counts as landed only when its dlt load is committed. A partial batch file left by a kill during the write is not read at start (adversarial round 2, C-3).
+rationale: Small enough that a crash re-reads little, large enough to avoid one Parquet file per event. The rejected option is one load per event, which produces many small files and slows the lake reads. The rejected option for the end trigger is a clean close of the body, because to an SSE client the end of a body is a dropped connection, so a clean close can come in the middle of a stream.
