+status: proposed
+question: How does the consumer resume after a drop or a stop without losing or double-counting an event?
+choice: After any end of the SSE body (a connection error or a clean close), the consumer reconnects with Last-Event-ID, until DURATION expires, SIGINT, or the replay sends its end signal (see cd-1-stream-micro-batch). A reconnect attempt fails on a connection error or refusal, an HTTP status other than 200 or 204, or a clean close that delivers no event with a new meta_id. A failed attempt does not end the run. The consumer counts consecutive failed attempts, resets the count when a reconnect delivers a new event, and writes the count to its exit line. The reconnect sends the last SSE id the process consumed, which is kept in memory. The reconnect waits the SSE retry value, or 1 second when none is sent, through an injected sleep. The process keeps one set of meta_id values, loaded at start with the meta_id values already in <lake-root>/stream_edit_raw/, and holding every meta_id it has consumed from start to stop. It drops a later copy of one: a dropped copy takes no arrival_seq and does not move the watermark, so the first arrival's is_late stands. Each run takes --lake-root (default lake/). The cursor file is <lake-root>/_stream_cursor.json and records the last landed SSE id. Per micro-batch: write the batch file first, then write the cursor file under a temp name and rename it with os.replace. The start load of the set is the dedupe against meta_id already in the lake. Phase 4 verifies that the replay honors Last-Event-ID and records the form of each disconnect.
+rationale: The batch-then-cursor order means a crash between the two re-reads some events, and the dedupe removes them. The in-memory consumed set covers the open batch, which a lake-only dedupe misses, so an in-process reconnect does not write an event twice. The consumed set also covers the since fallback, because a reconnect that resends from the start is dropped by the same set. The rejected option is cursor-first, which can lose a batch on a crash. The rejected option for the in-process case is a lake-only dedupe. If the replay does not honor Last-Event-ID, the consumer uses the since parameter with the last event time, and the consumed set and the lake dedupe still hold. A clean close is a disconnect, not an end: the replay's end signal is the only clean end, so a clean close in the middle of a stream does not drop events. A bounded failure exit (five failed attempts) would end a run before DURATION with status 0, and the spec says the stream is consumed for DURATION, so the run has no failure bound (adversarial round 1, B-1).
+---
+Ticket: cd-1
